Z||000000||DXR_DAD||07-12-2000||02:27 PM||||just got a Dewalt DW849 buffer 1000 to 3000 RPM, speed control. I think the price was right. I've priced this one at about <BR>$269.00??  I got it for $216.00.<P>Just wanted to know froms the pros!!<BR>how is this machaine is and what about that price? And some tips on how to use  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>THANKS<P>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 07-12-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-12-2000||08:43 PM||||The first big variable speed buffer/polisher I got (Sears) I managed to burn the paint on my hood (this was before Fiero).  It takes some skill to use a buffer properly.  When you get the technique down, you can get a real nice shine.  Just keep it moving.  Keep the buffing bonnet clean !<P>Now I use an inexpensive random orbital buffer with a foam bonnet to put on cleaners, polishes and waxes.  The use old elbow grease and lots of cotten rags to wipe and buff out.||||reg||3
Z||000002||snowkttn||07-13-2000||01:21 AM||||I am like Old Lar.......I bought a cheap brand polisher and love it....beats all that elbow work in polishing.  Makes waxing a breeeeezzzzeeeeee  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">))))||||reg||6
